## Solution

**Claim.** The polynomials satisfying the condition are exactly $P(t)=\alpha t^{2}+\beta t^{4}$ with $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}$.

### 1. First consequences: $P(0)=0$ and $P$ is even

Set $x=a-b,\ y=b-c,\ z=c-a$, so $x+y+z=0$, and let $s=a+b+c$. Since
$$a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2}=s^{2}-2(ab+bc+ca)=s^{2},$$
and
$$x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=2(a^{2}+b^{2}+c^{2})-2(ab+bc+ca)=2s^{2},$$
we get $s^{2}=\tfrac12(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2})$.

Conversely, for any $x,y,z$ with $x+y+z=0$ and any $s$ with $s^{2}=\tfrac12(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2})$, the system $a-b=x,\ b-c=y,\ c-a=z,\ a+b+c=s$ has the real solution
$$a=\tfrac{s+2x+y}{3},\quad b=\tfrac{s-x+y}{3},\quad c=\tfrac{s-x-2y}{3},$$
and a direct check gives $ab+bc+ca=0$. Both signs of $s$ are admissible, so the functional equation yields $P(s)=P(-s)$ for every real $s$ (taking $a=s,b=0,c=0$ shows every real $s$ is attained). Hence **$P$ is even**.

Taking $a=s,b=0,c=0$ in the original equation: $P(s)+P(0)+P(-s)=2P(s)$, i.e. $2P(s)+P(0)=2P(s)$, so **$P(0)=0$**.

### 2. Reduction to a polynomial identity

Since $P$ is even with $P(0)=0$, write $P(t)=Q(t^{2})$ with $Q(0)=0$, say $Q(u)=\sum_{n\ge 1}a_{n}u^{n}$. The equation becomes, for all $x,y,z$ with $x+y+z=0$,
$$Q(x^{2})+Q(y^{2})+Q(z^{2})=2Q\!\Big(\tfrac{x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}}{2}\Big).$$
Setting $z=-x-y$ and using $x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}=2(x^{2}+xy+y^{2})$:
$$\boxed{\,Q(x^{2})+Q(y^{2})+Q\big((x+y)^{2}\big)=2Q(x^{2}+xy+y^{2})\quad\text{for all }x,y.\,}\tag{E}$$

### 3. Homogeneity forces a monomial-by-monomial check

Each term $a_{n}u^{n}$ contributes only homogeneous degree $2n$ in $(x,y)$. Equating homogeneous components of degree $2n$ in (E):
$$a_{n}\big[x^{2n}+y^{2n}+(x+y)^{2n}\big]=2a_{n}(x^{2}+xy+y^{2})^{n}.$$
Thus for each $n\ge 1$, either $a_{n}=0$, or the identity
$$x^{2n}+y^{2n}+(x+y)^{2n}=2(x^{2}+xy+y^{2})^{n}\tag{$I_n$}$$
holds for all $x,y$.

### 4. Determining which $n$ work

**$n=1$:** $x^{2}+y^{2}+(x+y)^{2}=2x^{2}+2y^{2}+2xy=2(x^{2}+xy+y^{2})$. ✓

**$n=2$:** $(x+y)^{4}=x^{4}+4x^{3}y+6x^{2}y^{2}+4xy^{3}+y^{4}$, so
$$\text{LHS}=2x^{4}+2y^{4}+4x^{3}y+6x^{2}y^{2}+4xy^{3}.$$
Also $(x^{2}+xy+y^{2})^{2}=x^{4}+y^{4}+3x^{2}y^{2}+2x^{3}y+2xy^{3}$, so
$$\text{RHS}=2x^{4}+2y^{4}+4x^{3}y+6x^{2}y^{2}+4xy^{3}=\text{LHS}.\ \checkmark$$

**$n\ge 3$:** Set $y=x$ in ($I_n$): $2x^{2n}+(2x)^{2n}=2(3x^{2})^{n}$, i.e.
$$2+4^{n}=2\cdot 3^{n}.\tag{*}$$
For $n=1,2$ this holds ($6=6$, $18=18$). For $n\ge 3$, $\big(\tfrac43\big)^{n}\ge\big(\tfrac43\big)^{3}=\tfrac{64}{27}>2$, so $4^{n}>2\cdot 3^{n}$, hence $2+4^{n}>2\cdot 3^{n}$ and ($*$) fails. Therefore ($I_n$) fails for every $n\ge 3$, forcing $a_{n}=0$.

### 5. Conclusion

Only $n=1$ and $n=2$ survive, so $Q(u)=a_{1}u+a_{2}u^{2}$, i.e.
$$P(t)=a_{1}t^{2}+a_{2}t^{4}.$$

Both monomials $t^{2}$ and $t^{4}$ were verified to satisfy ($I_n$), hence (E), and therefore the original equation (the reduction steps are reversible). Thus every polynomial of the form $P(t)=\alpha t^{2}+\beta t^{4}$ works, and these are all of them.

**The polynomials are exactly $\;P(t)=\alpha t^{2}+\beta t^{4}\;$ for $\alpha,\beta\in\mathbb{R}$.**

